WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.400
In the ever-expanding DC Extended Universe,&nbsp;
there are a number of storylines, plot points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.760
and narrative threads that have disappeared&nbsp;
without explanation. It doesn't mean they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.240
never return, of course, but for now these are all&nbsp;
major unfinished business for the growing DCEU.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:25.040
The film that kicked off the DCEU as we know it&nbsp;
was "Man of Steel." It followed the evolution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.720
one Clark Kent, played by Henry Cavill, from&nbsp;
small town American alien boy to becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.560
Superman. In the process he faces villains from&nbsp;
his long lost Kryptonian heritage. Primarily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.120
General Zod, played by Michael Shannon,&nbsp;
and his renegade soldiers after they escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.320
from imprisonment in the Phantom Zone. Zod's&nbsp;
genocidal plan was to use The World Engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.720
Kryptonian technology that can terraform a world&nbsp;
into one inhabitable for Kryptonians, on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.400
The result would be an Earth remade in the image&nbsp;
of Krypton and suitable for Kryptonian life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:04.240
but at the cost of its existing life. Zod's&nbsp;
own ship, the Black Zero, contributed towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.760
the terraforming on one end of the planet with&nbsp;
the World Engine sitting over the Indian Ocean.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.960
Here's the thing: the terraforming process would&nbsp;
affect all life on Earth including the oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.200
and the World Engine was operating&nbsp;
directly over the Indian Ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.440
We know from DC history and "Aquaman" that the&nbsp;
Atlanteans are extremely sensitive to changes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.720
or mistreatment of the world's oceans.&nbsp;
Also, they have strong weaponry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.160
technologically advanced vehicles. Given what&nbsp;
we know, there's no reason why they wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.120
respond to the World Engine, perceiving it as&nbsp;
an attack against their seas and sovereignty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.480
While Superman did halt its efforts somewhat&nbsp;
quickly, it's surprising they didn't react.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.240
Where were they that was more pressing&nbsp;
than this terraforming attack?

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:58.160
"Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice" is the first&nbsp;
DCEU film to incorporate the Dark Knight himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.520
folding Batman in after he's spent 20 years&nbsp;
fighting crime in Gotham. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.960
Batman, played by Ben Affleck, is a hardened&nbsp;
and more violent vigilante—even taking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.160
branding criminals, and he works alone with&nbsp;
the help of Alfred. When we see the Batcave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.960
one of its more haunting pieces is a&nbsp;
damaged Robin costume in a glass case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:24.320
with "HAHAHA JOKE'S ON YOU BATMAN"&nbsp;
spray-painted across the front. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.960
are bullet holes in the suit, but there's&nbsp;
little in the film to explain its backstory.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.760
We know a few key pieces of backstory from&nbsp;
subsequent comments and films. Harley Quinn's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.880
introduction in "Suicide Squad" lists her as an&nbsp;
accomplice to Robin's murder. This admittedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.400
causes problems for the intended timelines of&nbsp;
both Snyder and "Suicide Squad" director David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.520
Ayer. Joker has his gold grill in his mouth upon&nbsp;
meeting Harley, but it is said to have appeared as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:55.520
a result of Batman beating him in retaliation for&nbsp;
Robin's death. If that's the case, when and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.480
did the Joker actually kill Robin, and what&nbsp;
was Harley's actual relation to that crime?

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.880
Talking about the lack of clarity&nbsp;
over Robin's death in the DCEU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.480
highlights exactly how little we know&nbsp;
about Batman's 20-year history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.960
fighting crime in Gotham. In a telling&nbsp;
line to Alfred, Bruce Wayne laments,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.840
"20 years in Gotham, Alfred. We've&nbsp;
seen what promises are worth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.120
How many good guys are left?&nbsp;
How many stay that way?"

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:28.480
The line suggests that fellow vigilantes have&nbsp;
fallen, like Robin, and perhaps turned to crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.680
In "Suicide Squad" we see Killer Croc and watch&nbsp;
the Caped Crusader chase Joker and Harley Quinn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.480
and elsewhere intervene to capture Deadshot.&nbsp;
But other than some vague generalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.200
we know almost nothing about Batman's&nbsp;
history in Gotham or with these villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.960
nor the vigilantes that have supported his&nbsp;
crusade. Plus, most of the DCEU has moved on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.560
instead of clarifying any of these omissions.&nbsp;
What's the history of this Batman and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.760
allies and conflicts? How did it contribute&nbsp;
towards his actions in "Batman v Superman"?

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.200
“You’re out of your mind, Bruce Wayne!”

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.520
In "Justice League," we see the&nbsp;
first war between Earth's forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.280
and the forces of Darkseid that took place&nbsp;
5,000 years ago. Regardless of the version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.480
both showed a Green Lantern fighting alongside&nbsp;
Earth's forces, then dying in the battle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.480
its power ring flying off to find a new Lantern.&nbsp;
Lanterns essentially protect sectors of space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.080
and will intervene in situations on a&nbsp;
planet they specialize in protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:31.840
from extraterrestrial threats, invasions, and&nbsp;
other space-based attackers. Most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.440
sectors shouldn't go without a protector, which&nbsp;
is why the ring sought a new Lantern immediately.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.960
So, where were the Lanterns over the last 5,000&nbsp;
years? Where were the Lanterns when Zod and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.520
Kryptonians attacked Earth in "Man of Steel"?&nbsp;
Where were they when Doomsday emerged in "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.360
v Superman," or especially in "Justice League"&nbsp;
when another extraterrestrial invasion occurred?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.400
These are exactly the sorts of interstellar&nbsp;
conflicts that Green Lanterns are supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.360
to respond to. And, while we see Green&nbsp;
Lanterns in an upcoming HBO Max series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.440
it makes little sense that they'd sit out&nbsp;
these subsequent interstellar assaults.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.720
In "Shazam!" the DCEU opens up&nbsp;
its magical side. In the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.720
the Rock of Eternity is said to be the&nbsp;
source of all magic as well as the place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.360
where the Seven Deadly Sins are kept. The&nbsp;
original wizard Shazam and his seven family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.200
members formed the Council of Wizards there. At&nbsp;
some point in the past, Shazam gave Teth-Adam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.120
later known as Black Adam, powers in that realm.&nbsp;
The conflict pitting Black Adam and the unleashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.480
Seven Deadly Sins against the Council of Wizards&nbsp;
resulted in the deaths of most of the latter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:44.160
Considerable time passed before Shazam transferred&nbsp;
his powers to Billy Batson and later Billy's own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:49.760
Shazam Family to fight the newly released Seven&nbsp;
Deadly Sins and the villainous Dr. Sivana.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.920
Certainly a lot will be explained about Black&nbsp;
Adam in subsequent films. But in the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.080
he began life as an Egyptian slave, putting&nbsp;
the events that killed most of the Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.840
of Wizards approximately around 5,000 years&nbsp;
ago. This is exactly when the Atlanteans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:09.200
Amazons, and early humans fought the forces of&nbsp;
Darkseid in "Justice League." If the Council of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.800
Wizards were around, where were they when evil&nbsp;
alien gods were invading? Gods, Atlanteans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:20.880
and Amazons were all there, yet the Wizards didn't&nbsp;
show up to protect the Earth at this pivotal time.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.240
In "Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice,"&nbsp;
Lex Luthor, played by Jesse Eisenberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.400
wages a multi-pronged attack on&nbsp;
Superman. It involved many steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.720
from blowing up the United States Capital&nbsp;
to kidnapping Superman's mom. In a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:41.040
of justification he claims that his motivation is&nbsp;
basically being against the notion of 'gods' on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.800
Earth. He argues that devils don't come from hell&nbsp;
beneath us, rather they come from the sky. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:53.280
clearly he sees Superman as an evil enemy. He also&nbsp;
shares his interesting argument of a divine being.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.240
"If God is all-powerful, He cannot be all good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.600
And if He is all good, then&nbsp;
He cannot be all-powerful."

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.960
Ultimately, We aren't really given other reasons&nbsp;
for Luthor to hate Superman. In the comics Luthor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:12.000
is said to have a long history and personal&nbsp;
vendetta against him. Not so here. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.680
reason we have is Luthor's supposed disdain&nbsp;
for "gods" that have power above humans.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.680
Luthor's final plan beyond his convoluted mess of&nbsp;
tactics involves mixing his own blood with Zod's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:30.320
body, combining the two with Kryptonian technology&nbsp;
to create Doomsday, the ultra-powerful behemoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:35.360
that kills Superman. So his motivating&nbsp;
factor is apparently a hatred of gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.680
and yet his final plan is to&nbsp;
engineer a god that's more powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:45.120
more animalistic, and less subject to human&nbsp;
control? That's not what a brilliant planner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.400
would do with that motivation, so what's Luthor's&nbsp;
real incentive for absolutely hating Superman?

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.800
Regarding Luthor's engineering of the&nbsp;
Doomsday monster in "Batman v Superman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.520
Dawn of Justice," the process makes relatively&nbsp;
little sense. Lex enters the Genesis Chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.760
of the Kryptonian scout ship using Zod's&nbsp;
fingerprints and command key. He's taught the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.280
wealth of knowledge from the Kryptonian archives,&nbsp;
learning of an ancient Kryptonian monster — aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:15.200
Doomsday — that he re-creates as a failsafe if&nbsp;
Batman can't or doesn't kill Superman. The process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.480
involves bringing Zod's corpse into the Genesis&nbsp;
Chamber, then Luthor cutting himself and bleeding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:26.000
into the Chamber and onto Zod. The resultant&nbsp;
engineering feat is a version of the recreation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:32.400
Doomsday. It’s an effectively mindless creature&nbsp;
with immense strength, at least on par with if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.760
stronger than Superman, boundless rage,&nbsp;
and some serious regenerative abilities.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:44.480
What makes little sense is why corrupting&nbsp;
Kryptonian DNA with human DNA made such a strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:50.720
if not stronger entity than Superman. Humans&nbsp;
look like Kryptonians in every relevant way, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:56.400
Doomsday is larger than either and looks like&nbsp;
neither. Kryptonians are massively stronger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.760
on Earth than humans are, and Doomsday seems&nbsp;
stronger than Kryptonians despite the addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:06.240
of human DNA. Adding human DNA wouldn't&nbsp;
be the recipe to make this ancient beast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.040
because it's unlikely that its ancient&nbsp;
Kryptonian creator had humans lying around.&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:15.280
Why in the world would blending&nbsp;
Kryptonian DNA with human DNA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:21.809
not make Doomsday a hybrid of the two, thus&nbsp;
weaker than Superman? It makes little sense.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:25.040
In "Batman v. Superman: Dawn&nbsp;
of Justice," Bruce Wayne is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.760
woken from a nightmare by a vision of Barry Allen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.280
aka The Flash. He offers Bruce a warning about&nbsp;
the future—albeit vague and confusing—saying,

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:39.440
"Bruce! Listen to me now. It's Lois.&nbsp;
It's Lois Lane. She's the key."

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:50.000
"You were right about him. You've always been&nbsp;
right about him! Fear him! Find us, Bruce!"

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.760
In "Zack Snyder's Justice League,"&nbsp;
Superman succumbs to the Anti-Life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.400
equation and under Darkseid's rule&nbsp;
lays waste to many of his enemies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:04.240
The Flash, we can infer, went back in time to&nbsp;
warn Batman about Superman's inherent danger.

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.720
Of course this was a plot thread that the&nbsp;
DCEU didn't move forward with, so there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:13.760
high chance it will never be fulfilled and we only&nbsp;
see in part where it may have been intended to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:17.840
At the same time, the Flash sequence&nbsp;
still exists within DCEU continuity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.680
but it is unclear how they're going to try&nbsp;
to give it finality and meaning, if at all.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:28.400
In "Wonder Woman 1984," the FBI turn to Dr.&nbsp;
Barbara Minerva to aid in the identification of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:34.320
the wish-granting Dreamstone. Failing businessman&nbsp;
Maxwell Lord wishes to become the stone itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.800
It grants him the ability to gain power&nbsp;
every time someone makes a wish through him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.760
He uses this to eventually take over the White&nbsp;
House, secretive U.S. communications technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:48.160
and effectively the whole world as&nbsp;
he grants the wishes of humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:53.280
All these wishes come with a cost to their&nbsp;
users, of course, and it all pushes humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.560
nearly to the point of global nuclear war.&nbsp;
Wonder Woman uses her Lasso of Truth on Lord,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:04.080
convincing the world — including Lord — to&nbsp;
end the crisis by renouncing their wishes.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.640
But even though there's no indication that&nbsp;
renouncing wishes made anyone forget the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:14.560
events that just occurred on a global scale,&nbsp;
the movie just sort of moves on. If everyone on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:20.000
Earth was allowed to make wishes that came true,&nbsp;
causing massive global chaos, that would change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:26.320
human history, civilization, and religion as we&nbsp;
know it. It would reveal the existence of magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:32.880
which is a big deal, and publicly confirm the&nbsp;
existence of metahumans as early as the 1980s. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:39.040
would change literally everything in the DCEU, but&nbsp;
instead it was dropped like it wasn't a big deal.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.840
“That’s the truth.”

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.640
In "Suicide Squad," Amanda Waller builds a team&nbsp;
of expendable super-villains to go on dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:50.880
missions in the wake of Superman's death&nbsp;
in exchange for commuted prison sentences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:56.080
The main exception is Dr. June Moone, who is&nbsp;
possessed by an ancient interdimensional sorceress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.600
known as Enchantress. When Enchantress and her&nbsp;
ancient brother Incubus move to take over Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.240
the pair use magic to turn a variety of&nbsp;
people into their supernatural soldiers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:10.720
The odd thing about all this is that&nbsp;
the villains here are clearly magical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.720
mystical threats and Waller knows&nbsp;
this. Even still, most of the members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:19.840
of the “suicide squad” have literally no&nbsp;
mystical abilities or relation to magic.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:25.120
This is a major oversight, because the only reason&nbsp;
the team succeeds is due to the efforts of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:31.440
somewhat magical members. For example, El Diablo&nbsp;
uses his cursed full fire form to fight Incubus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:37.200
and push him over a bomb. Waller knows Enchantress&nbsp;
is a sorceress, so why send an ill-chosen team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:42.480
for a magical job? Was the U.S. government so&nbsp;
uninterested in magic that they never bothered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:48.800
have good contingency plans against magic attacks?&nbsp;
And have they gotten any better on that front?

